    <section class="section">
      <div class="container two-col">
        <div class="prose">
          <h2>Built for speed, not for stopping</h2>
          <p>The AWPR was built to take through-traffic off the roads inside Aberdeen, connecting the north and south sections of the A90 with the A96 in a sweep around the west of the city, past Westhill, Kingswells, Craibstone and Dyce. It's a genuinely different driving environment to the older roads it relieves: consistently high speeds, grade-separated junctions rather than roundabouts, and long gaps between exits. That makes it efficient for getting around Aberdeen, but it also means fewer natural stopping points if something goes wrong with your vehicle.</p>

          <h2>Spotting trouble early matters more here</h2>
          <p>Because laybys and hard-shoulder-style refuges are limited on the AWPR compared with slower roads, the moment you notice a warning sign — a dashboard light, an unusual noise, a loss of power, or the car pulling to one side — start planning your next move immediately. If the vehicle is still driveable and it's safe to do so, ease off and aim for the next junction, slip road or service point rather than pressing on and hoping the problem resolves itself. Don't wait until you're forced to stop in a live lane to start looking for somewhere safe.</p>

          <h2>If you do have to stop on the main carriageway</h2>
          <p>Sometimes there's no choice. If your vehicle stops moving on the open sections of the AWPR, get as far left as possible, ideally onto any verge or hard strip available, and put your hazard lights on immediately so following traffic has warning at speed. Once stopped, if you can get out and away from the vehicle safely — behind a barrier or well down the verge, clear of the carriageway — that's safer than remaining inside a car that's still close to fast-moving traffic. Never attempt a roadside repair or try to push the vehicle yourself in a live lane; wait for us or for a recovery vehicle designed to work safely at the roadside.</p>

          <h2>Finding you quickly on a bypass</h2>
          <p>Because the AWPR loops around several different areas — Westhill and Kingswells to the south-west, Craibstone and Dyce to the north — a road name alone doesn't tell us much about where you are. When you call, give us the nearest junction number if you can see a sign, or a recognisable landmark such as Westhill, Kingswells, Craibstone or the Dyce/A96 junction, along with the direction you were travelling. That's the fastest way to get a recovery vehicle to the right spot.</p>

          <div class="callout">
            <p><strong>On a fast, open bypass like the AWPR:</strong> get off the running lanes and onto a verge, slip road or junction where possible, keep hazard lights on at all times, get well clear of the vehicle and behind any barrier if it's still close to traffic, and never attempt a repair in a live lane. Give us your nearest junction or landmark and direction of travel when you call.</p>
          </div>

          <h2>Not a breakdown — a collision?</h2>
          <p>If your vehicle has been damaged in a collision on the AWPR rather than broken down mechanically, our <a href="../services/accident-recovery-aberdeen.html">accident recovery</a> service is set up to handle that. Let us know it was a collision when you call.</p>
        </div>

